WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.087
<v Bev>Go on, Brian. Just a hint. Is the next one any good?

00:00:05.467 --> 00:00:08.630
<v Brian>A hint is a score, Bev. The score's in the magazine.

00:00:08.804 --> 00:00:11.155
<v Bev>He's like this all the time, isn't he?

00:00:11.553 --> 00:00:14.731
<v Gareth>Every single day. We wouldn't have him any other way.

00:00:24.531 --> 00:00:31.753
<v Bev>Hello there, it's Bev, the kettle's on, and this is Dalex After Hours, the slot where we find out how computer games are really made.

00:00:32.353 --> 00:00:37.294
<v Bev>Every fact on this show comes out of the book the team's writing, and from the games themselves.

00:00:37.894 --> 00:00:39.860
<v Bev>Gareth's across the desk as always.

00:00:39.931 --> 00:00:41.939
<v Gareth>Hiya, Bev. Hiya, everyone.

00:00:42.143 --> 00:00:52.184
<v Bev>And this week's a special, because our guest doesn't work at Dalex at all. He's walked over from the building across the courtyard. Brian Openshaw, editor of Ink and Paper, welcome.

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:59.323
<v Brian>Thank you, Bev. And before anyone asks, I'm independent of Dalex. I'm here as a guest, and I'll leave as one.

00:00:59.622 --> 00:01:01.353
<v Gareth>He said that on the stairs as well.

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:02.626
<v Brian>It bears repeating.

00:01:02.858 --> 00:01:09.940
<v Bev>Now Brian, the Proclaimers are at number three this week, with Letter from America. Marks out of a hundred?

00:01:10.035 --> 00:01:11.698
<v Brian>I don't review records, Bev.

00:01:11.663 --> 00:01:13.252
<v Bev>Go on. Just a hint.

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:21.079
<v Brian>A hint is a score. But I'll say it's a fair song, sung by two men who mean every word. Fair is the whole job.

00:01:21.283 --> 00:01:23.312
<v Gareth>That's the most we'll get out of him tonight.

00:01:26.612 --> 00:01:28.359
<v Bev>So you're not in this building at all.

00:01:28.644 --> 00:01:32.313
<v Brian>Across the courtyard, in another building, because critics always are.

00:01:32.547 --> 00:01:34.027
<v Bev>And what's on your desk over there?

00:01:34.359 --> 00:01:39.063
<v Brian>Proofs, a word processor and a red pen. And the masthead, in a typeface I chose myself.

00:01:39.227 --> 00:01:41.816
<v Gareth>Nobody else was allowed near that typeface.

00:01:41.758 --> 00:01:42.882
<v Brian>Nobody else asked.

00:01:43.037 --> 00:01:47.448
<v Bev>I'm told the scores are all yours. And that you're unimpressed on principle.

00:01:47.604 --> 00:01:56.981
<v Brian>So I'm told. I'd put it more kindly. I praise what a game earns, and I say so with a smile. What I won't do is hand out praise it hasn't earned.

00:01:57.149 --> 00:02:00.390
<v Bev>Ooh, Brian. Percy's just climbed onto your lap.

00:02:00.602 --> 00:02:02.238
<v Brian>I'm told I don't care for cats.

00:02:02.314 --> 00:02:03.706
<v Gareth>He's scratching his ears, Bev.

00:02:03.748 --> 00:02:05.109
<v Brian>This never happened.

00:02:05.363 --> 00:02:07.727
<v Bev>Is there anything over there you do love?

00:02:07.983 --> 00:02:13.070
<v Brian>There's a ZX printer on the side, with its silver paper. It smells of nineteen eighty-two.

00:02:13.281 --> 00:02:16.039
<v Gareth>He let me have a sniff once. He's right, you know.

00:02:16.224 --> 00:02:18.126
<v Brian>I'll neither confirm nor deny that.

00:02:18.353 --> 00:02:21.002
<v Bev>So what does a magazine do with a Dalex tape?

00:02:21.253 --> 00:02:27.914
<v Brian>We review the tape that actually shipped, and our scores are our own. Not a promise, not a preview. The tape.

00:02:28.061 --> 00:02:30.037
<v Bev>And how many of these have you done?

00:02:30.424 --> 00:02:41.975
<v Brian>All six Dalex games have had a review in the magazine. Antigravity has seventy-four per cent, Mower Mayhem and Tiger Gorilla seventy-two, Gold Master seventy-one, Wordris sixty-eight and WOLF sixty-four.

00:02:42.271 --> 00:02:45.200
<v Bev>Ooh. Not one of them over seventy-five.

00:02:45.429 --> 00:02:47.765
<v Brian>Not one. And no awards either.

00:02:48.007 --> 00:02:49.993
<v Gareth>Brian, tell her what the award's called.

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:55.124
<v Brian>The SPARKLER. It goes to a game at ninety per cent or more, with nothing serious left open.

00:02:55.287 --> 00:02:57.872
<v Bev>That's a lovely name for something nobody's won.

00:02:58.064 --> 00:02:59.703
<v Brian>It'll be lovelier when someone wins it.

00:02:59.841 --> 00:03:02.509
<v Bev>Right then, what's the one idea this week?

00:03:02.923 --> 00:03:10.570
<v Brian>A score is a middle mark. Three reviewers each take the tape that shipped, read the same evidence and give their marks, and the magazine prints the middle one.

00:03:10.811 --> 00:03:13.436
<v Bev>Why three? Why not just you?

00:03:13.834 --> 00:03:18.014
<v Brian>Because one reviewer is noisy. It's like judging a player on one go.

00:03:18.157 --> 00:03:28.880
<v Gareth>And the games learned that the hard way. Wordris had one reviewer at a time to start with. Its score went from sixty-six to seventy to fifty-seven, while every milestone was fixing things.

00:03:29.163 --> 00:03:31.115
<v Bev>It got better and the mark went down?

00:03:31.458 --> 00:03:37.379
<v Gareth>Down by thirteen, in a milestone that fixed things. One reviewer at a time, and the mark wobbles.

00:03:37.534 --> 00:03:47.421
<v Brian>So now each review has three, and we print the median. And here's the part I'm proudest of. Nobody gets to ask for the review to be done again in the hope of a better mark. That's ruled out.

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:49.489
<v Bev>Not even if Mo asks nicely?

00:03:49.844 --> 00:03:51.421
<v Brian>Mo knows better than to ask.

00:03:51.644 --> 00:03:56.046
<v Bev>And what do your reviewers actually look at? Do they come in and have a go?

00:03:56.360 --> 00:04:05.564
<v Brian>They never set foot in this building. They get the tape that shipped, the screenshots and the evidence, and nothing else. Nobody from Dalex gets to sit beside them and explain.

00:04:05.768 --> 00:04:13.040
<v Gareth>The evidence is everything that's been checked about that tape. The checks that ran, what the playtesters found and the real pictures off the screen.

00:04:13.218 --> 00:04:19.755
<v Brian>And every claim a reviewer prints carries a hidden label pointing at the evidence behind it. If the evidence doesn't show it, they can't say it.

00:04:19.699 --> 00:04:20.642
<v Bev>Hidden where?

00:04:20.949 --> 00:04:29.815
<v Gareth>In the reviewer's notes, kept on file with the review. The reader sees the sentence. Behind it, there's a list of which checks and which findings back it up.

00:04:29.909 --> 00:04:31.399
<v Bev>You said there'd be pencils.

00:04:31.638 --> 00:04:48.828
<v Gareth>There are. This is the actual Mower Mayhem review, from its sixth milestone. Three separate reviews, each written from scratch. Here are their three marks for sound. Write them down. Seventy-five. Eighty. Seventy-seven.

00:04:49.086 --> 00:04:52.929
<v Bev>Seventy-five, eighty, seventy-seven. Got them.

00:04:53.076 --> 00:04:58.664
<v Gareth>Now put them in order, smallest first, and take the one in the middle. If you want a go, pause me now.

00:05:01.785 --> 00:05:06.488
<v Bev>Seventy-five, seventy-seven, eighty. So the middle one's seventy-seven.

00:05:06.754 --> 00:05:09.260
<v Brian>And seventy-seven is what we printed for sound.

00:05:09.405 --> 00:05:11.983
<v Bev>Oh, that's tidy. Is that the average?

00:05:12.370 --> 00:05:27.514
<v Gareth>Good question, that. It's the one I asked when I started. No, the average would be a bit more than seventy-seven. The middle one doesn't care how high the eighty is. If that reviewer had loved it to bits and given it ninety-five, the middle would still be seventy-seven.

00:05:27.765 --> 00:05:30.221
<v Bev>So one keen reviewer can't run off with the score.

00:05:30.381 --> 00:05:40.116
<v Brian>Nor one grumpy one. Every category gets its own middle mark. Presentation, graphics, sound, playability, addictive qualities, and overall.

00:05:40.298 --> 00:05:42.901
<v Bev>And the words? Whose words go in?

00:05:43.245 --> 00:05:46.942
<v Brian>The words come from the one of the three reviews that sits closest to the middle marks.

00:05:47.124 --> 00:05:54.834
<v Gareth>Here's the other side of it. On all three, the overall came out the same. Seventy-two, seventy-two, seventy-two.

00:05:54.845 --> 00:05:56.091
<v Bev>So they agreed.

00:05:56.387 --> 00:06:00.827
<v Brian>On the overall, to the point. On sound they were five apart, which is why there are three of them.

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:03.578
<v Bev>Now you've got your own pencil job, haven't you, Brian?

00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:13.170
<v Brian>I have. Write one sentence about a game you've played. Any game. No superlatives. Not the best, not the greatest, not amazing.

00:06:13.364 --> 00:06:18.383
<v Bev>Oh, that's hard. Mower Mayhem is the best lawnmower game I've ever... oh.

00:06:18.334 --> 00:06:18.929
<v Brian>Try again.

00:06:19.126 --> 00:06:24.093
<v Bev>Mower Mayhem has a little mower that turns the moment you press a key, and it never runs over the hedgehog.

00:06:24.306 --> 00:06:28.837
<v Brian>That's a sentence I'd print. And it's true, which is more than most superlatives manage.

00:06:29.003 --> 00:06:30.697
<v Bev>Why are you so set against them?

00:06:31.082 --> 00:06:38.235
<v Brian>Because a superlative is a claim. The best lawnmower game ever made. Have you played every lawnmower game ever made?

00:06:38.450 --> 00:06:39.402
<v Bev>I've played the one.

00:06:39.582 --> 00:06:45.290
<v Brian>Then you can't print it. Not one unearned superlative. That's the rule on my desk.

00:06:45.419 --> 00:06:48.599
<v Gareth>Have a go at my code, Bev. No superlatives.

00:06:48.884 --> 00:06:52.133
<v Bev>Gareth's code is very small and it does a lot of things.

00:06:52.405 --> 00:06:54.990
<v Brian>Very is doing no work there. Cut it.

00:06:55.218 --> 00:06:58.252
<v Bev>Gareth's code is small and does a lot of things.

00:06:58.458 --> 00:07:00.568
<v Brian>Better. Now tell me how small.

00:07:00.807 --> 00:07:05.812
<v Gareth>Four instructions in five bytes, the routine she means. Called from fifty-three places.

00:07:06.105 --> 00:07:09.273
<v Brian>And now it's a sentence with evidence in it. That's a review.

00:07:11.773 --> 00:07:15.974
<v Bev>So this is Mower Mayhem playing now. What did the reviewers say about it?

00:07:16.328 --> 00:07:27.531
<v Brian>There are three names on that page. Gaz Pocket, who wants the controls to answer. Prue Fairley, who likes a game to be fair. And Norm Crabtree, who notices the loading and the tunes.

00:07:27.797 --> 00:07:29.915
<v Bev>They sound like a family at Christmas.

00:07:29.972 --> 00:07:31.521
<v Gareth>They argue like one, too.

00:07:31.661 --> 00:07:39.035
<v Brian>Gaz opens with this. The mower turns the instant you touch Q A O P, and two keys at once take it round the diagonals.

00:07:39.299 --> 00:07:47.548
<v Gareth>That one's backed by a real check. Vik's test rig taps a key thirty times at odd moments, and every tap has to move the mower within one update.

00:07:47.762 --> 00:07:54.473
<v Brian>Then Gaz has his grumble. Stop by a weed and fire the trimmer, and the yellow beam shoots up a whisker beside the weed rather than through it.

00:07:54.602 --> 00:07:58.059
<v Bev>Oh, I've seen that. It still gets the weed, mind.

00:07:58.183 --> 00:08:03.436
<v Brian>He says so. The weed still goes pop, but it looks wonky. He's fair even when he moans.

00:08:03.406 --> 00:08:04.247
<v Bev>And Prue?

00:08:04.589 --> 00:08:16.643
<v Brian>Prue likes how fair it is. Nothing on the lawn ever ends your go. The cat swerves round you, the hedgehog shuffles off, and if an animal is in the beam's path the trimmer won't fire and shows a red exclamation mark instead.

00:08:16.885 --> 00:08:19.536
<v Bev>Good. Nobody wants a hurt hedgehog.

00:08:19.712 --> 00:08:24.655
<v Brian>And Norm, bless him, starts with the loading. Well over two minutes to load, so put the kettle on.

00:08:24.872 --> 00:08:26.379
<v Bev>A man after my own heart.

00:08:26.645 --> 00:08:28.352
<v Gareth>That line's on the advert as well.

00:08:28.590 --> 00:08:30.935
<v Bev>Dalex put a complaint on its own advert?

00:08:31.285 --> 00:08:35.930
<v Brian>The advert may only quote what a reviewer wrote, word for word. So that's what it quotes.

00:08:36.153 --> 00:08:38.460
<v Bev>And what did the box marks come to?

00:08:38.707 --> 00:08:51.123
<v Brian>Gaz gave it sixty-nine, Prue seventy-two and Norm seventy-four. Seventy-two overall. And the verdict: fair, friendly and cheap as chips, a cut above most budget fare, even if the trimmer's aim is a blade off.

00:08:51.397 --> 00:08:53.532
<v Bev>A blade off.  Did you write that pun?

00:08:53.781 --> 00:08:54.973
<v Brian>I'll be the judge of that.

00:08:54.967 --> 00:08:56.735
<v Gareth>That's editor for no comment.

00:08:57.512 --> 00:09:00.222
<v Bev>Are there rules about what a reviewer can say?

00:09:00.482 --> 00:09:08.257
<v Brian>Plenty. The page talks like a player, and a player doesn't know how the game was made or checked. So nothing about that goes on the page.

00:09:08.533 --> 00:09:13.340
<v Gareth>So no code, no routine names, no checksums, no milestones on the printed page.

00:09:13.603 --> 00:09:17.162
<v Bev>Not even the clever bits? Gareth would be heartbroken.

00:09:17.420 --> 00:09:22.016
<v Brian>Gareth can be heartbroken in his own building. The reader bought a game, not a lecture.

00:09:21.988 --> 00:09:23.060
<v Gareth>Fair.

00:09:23.283 --> 00:09:25.444
<v Bev>So how does a reviewer talk, then?

00:09:25.799 --> 00:09:35.613
<v Brian>Like a player. Informal and British. Brill, fab, ace, naff, a right pain, dead tricky. One or two of those a paragraph, mind, not a costume.

00:09:35.654 --> 00:09:38.525
<v Bev>Naff! My mum says naff.

00:09:38.790 --> 00:09:52.354
<v Brian>Your mum has a good ear. First person, as a player. My thumbs ached after Lawn three. And they talk about what a player would notice. Colour clash, flicker, the tune, sluggish keys, being sent back to the start, value for money.

00:09:52.479 --> 00:09:55.812
<v Gareth>And the loading time. That comes from the tape's real size.

00:09:55.971 --> 00:10:00.327
<v Brian>Norm's two minutes didn't come from his imagination. The evidence gives him the load.

00:10:00.617 --> 00:10:03.845
<v Bev>So where does all the honesty go, if it can't go on the page?

00:10:04.153 --> 00:10:15.070
<v Brian>Into the notes behind it. Every open finding, from severity one to three, has to appear on the page, in a player's words. And anything a reviewer couldn't check goes on a list of things not claimed.

00:10:15.308 --> 00:10:24.889
<v Gareth>Mower Mayhem's list has one about joysticks. The Ready card mentions a joystick, but no joystick was ever used in play. So the review says nothing about joysticks at all.

00:10:25.033 --> 00:10:27.952
<v Bev>It doesn't say it works and it doesn't say it doesn't.

00:10:28.192 --> 00:10:37.435
<v Brian>It says nothing, and the notes say why. And there's another on that list. How the game feels at full speed. The play was judged slowed down, so nobody claims to know.

00:10:37.410 --> 00:10:38.348
<v Bev>Slowed down?

00:10:38.632 --> 00:10:44.877
<v Gareth>The playtesters play it on the test rig, slowed down. That's what the evidence covers, so that's all the review can say.

00:10:45.080 --> 00:10:47.649
<v Bev>What stops a reviewer being too kind?

00:10:47.904 --> 00:11:00.332
<v Brian>Arithmetic. The overall has to stay within ten of the average of the other five. And if a game has an open severity one, a crash or a lock up or controls that don't work, the overall can't go above fifty-nine.

00:11:00.506 --> 00:11:03.074
<v Bev>Fifty-nine. Whatever the graphics are like?

00:11:03.452 --> 00:11:07.700
<v Brian>Whatever the graphics are like. A game cannot ship a crash with a good mark.

00:11:07.844 --> 00:11:17.028
<v Bev>Now hang on. In Gold Master, every game you ship earns a review mark. I've seen a screenshot of one getting eighty-nine per cent from Ink and Paper!

00:11:17.311 --> 00:11:26.788
<v Brian>That's a mark inside Gold Master, Bev. A game about this office, marking the pretend games you finish in it. Gold Master's own review, the real one, is seventy-one.

00:11:27.020 --> 00:11:29.356
<v Bev>So I'm not allowed to frame that eighty-nine.

00:11:29.510 --> 00:11:32.303
<v Brian>Frame it by all means. Just don't quote me.

00:11:32.583 --> 00:11:34.752
<v Gareth>The verdict on Gold Master's a good one, though.

00:11:34.940 --> 00:11:38.453
<v Brian>Not quite solid gold, but this busy little office game is well worth a copy.

00:11:38.701 --> 00:11:40.960
<v Bev>Oh, I like that. That's fair.

00:11:41.211 --> 00:11:44.342
<v Brian>Fair is the whole job. I may have said that already.

00:11:44.414 --> 00:11:45.498
<v Bev>Once or twice.

00:11:46.098 --> 00:11:48.286
<v Bev>Has a review ever changed a game?

00:11:48.647 --> 00:11:56.927
<v Brian>More than once, and I mean that as a compliment to the team. Our reviewers marked Tiger Gorilla's graphics down because a whole red tree trunk turned yellow behind the gorilla.

00:11:56.878 --> 00:11:57.969
<v Bev>Why would it do that?

00:11:58.302 --> 00:12:11.122
<v Gareth>Because of how the Spectrum does colour. Every little eight by eight square of the screen has two colours, an ink and a paper. A sprite colours the squares it covers by setting their ink. And the trunk's body was drawn in ink.

00:12:11.339 --> 00:12:14.163
<v Bev>So the gorilla walks past and paints the tree yellow.

00:12:14.417 --> 00:12:27.877
<v Gareth>Exactly that. So the fix was to store every bit of scenery that's mostly ink the other way round. The pixels flipped, and ink and paper swapped. It looks the same, but now the gorilla only recolours the detail, and the trunk keeps its red.

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:30.143
<v Bev>Lovely. Job done.

00:12:30.442 --> 00:12:45.740
<v Gareth>Not quite. That fix caused the next bug. Some tiles next to each other now held the same two colours the other way round. And when the scrolling camera sat half a square along, two halves shared one square, and one half came out in its colours swapped.

00:12:45.931 --> 00:12:47.303
<v Bev>What would I see on the telly?

00:12:47.659 --> 00:12:51.967
<v Gareth>A flash at the foot of every trunk, about six times a second, while you walked.

00:12:52.120 --> 00:12:59.830
<v Brian>Which is why I say a review tracks the open list, not the fixes behind it. Mower Mayhem once fixed all nine things the previous review complained about.

00:13:00.087 --> 00:13:03.285
<v Bev>All nine! That must have scored well.

00:13:03.485 --> 00:13:09.806
<v Brian>Later rounds opened new ones. The next reviewers marked down exactly the twelve still open, and scored it sixty-seven again.

00:13:09.813 --> 00:13:10.933
<v Bev>Oh, that's tough.

00:13:11.059 --> 00:13:17.030
<v Brian>Tough, but fair. A player doesn't care what you fixed last month. They care what's on the tape in front of them.

00:13:17.249 --> 00:13:25.625
<v Gareth>And we take it in good heart, Bev. A review like that is a list of exactly where to look next. Everyone on the floor reads it, and we sort it out together.

00:13:25.895 --> 00:13:34.277
<v Brian>And that's the right way round, if I may say so. You don't argue with a review. You go and change the game, and they did. Credit where it's earned.

00:13:34.534 --> 00:13:46.559
<v Gareth>And it works the other way. The weaknesses in a review are the findings the team chose to leave for later, written the way a player would say them. In that Mower Mayhem review, nothing the team left for later escaped a reviewer's eye.

00:13:46.807 --> 00:13:50.617
<v Bev>Give me one of those, then. A finding, the way a player says it.

00:13:50.903 --> 00:14:03.149
<v Brian>Prue's. Gliding at an angle, I had a flashing weed dead ahead, and the trimmer refused because of a hedgehog sitting off on the diagonal, nowhere near the line to the weed. Baffling, but it never cost me the lawn.

00:14:03.447 --> 00:14:10.572
<v Gareth>And underneath, that's a severity two finding the team decided to leave for later. Same bug. Prue's words.

00:14:10.796 --> 00:14:13.330
<v Bev>So the review's like a mirror for the to do list.

00:14:13.531 --> 00:14:16.864
<v Brian>That's a better sentence than most I get sent. Much obliged.

00:14:20.164 --> 00:14:24.318
<v Bev>Go on then, crystal ball time. Brian, will there still be magazines in the future?

00:14:24.722 --> 00:14:34.628
<v Brian>I'd like to think so. But suppose one day every home has a computer joined to every other one, down the telephone line. Anyone could print a review for the whole world to read, the same afternoon.

00:14:34.644 --> 00:14:37.074
<v Bev>Anyone? Without an editor?

00:14:37.370 --> 00:14:41.034
<v Brian>Without an editor. Which is why someone would still need to take the middle mark.

00:14:41.283 --> 00:14:51.634
<v Gareth>And suppose there's a language for those computers, call it Python, after Monty Python. You'd hand it the three marks, ask for the median, and it would sort them and give you the middle one. It'd come built in.

00:14:51.933 --> 00:14:56.631
<v Bev>Like on Tomorrow's World. Computers that do medians and a Monty Python language.

00:14:56.916 --> 00:14:59.048
<v Brian>I'll be sceptical until I see the evidence.

00:14:59.028 --> 00:15:00.593
<v Gareth>It's a guess, Brian.

00:15:00.784 --> 00:15:02.299
<v Brian>Then I'll score it when it ships.

00:15:02.460 --> 00:15:04.988
<v Bev>What should folk do before next week?

00:15:05.251 --> 00:15:13.884
<v Gareth>In the book the team's writing, there's a chapter called Playtesting, Triage and Keeping a Decisions Log. Its last exercise is called Map a review.

00:15:13.905 --> 00:15:14.409
<v Bev>Go on.

00:15:14.551 --> 00:15:24.176
<v Gareth>You take Mower Mayhem's review from its sixth milestone, and you match each reviewer's opinion to the findings the playtesters left open. Then look for a complaint that has nothing behind it.

00:15:24.451 --> 00:15:26.315
<v Brian>And if you find one, write to me.

00:15:26.522 --> 00:15:31.406
<v Bev>And for the pencil, Brian's sentence. One sentence about a game, no superlatives.

00:15:31.647 --> 00:15:34.759
<v Gareth>Go on then. It's harder than it sounds.

00:15:34.783 --> 00:15:36.073
<v Brian>It's meant to be.

00:15:40.073 --> 00:15:48.080
<v Bev>That's us for this week. United have been playing friendlies in Bermuda, and won four one, twice. Brian, any marks for that?

00:15:48.433 --> 00:15:50.293
<v Brian>I'd want to know who scored the sunshine.

00:15:50.509 --> 00:15:55.154
<v Bev>And City won two nil at Bournemouth on Tuesday, so the sky blue desks are happy too.

00:15:55.754 --> 00:16:05.907
<v Bev>Next time it's another special, and it's Simon, who paints the loading screens and the covers. If you've got a question for the team, or for Brian, send it in, and we'll put it to them.

00:16:06.507 --> 00:16:07.643
<v Bev>Thanks, Brian.

00:16:07.905 --> 00:16:12.453
<v Brian>Thank you, Bev. Much obliged. And Gareth, the answer's still no.

00:16:12.414 --> 00:16:13.961
<v Gareth>I hadn't asked anything!

00:16:13.919 --> 00:16:15.148
<v Brian>You were going to.

00:16:15.245 --> 00:16:16.476
<v Bev>Right then, turn it up.

00:16:16.765 --> 00:16:30.290
<v Daniel>This is Daniel Vaughan, who made the show. That was nineteen eighty-seven. Brian's guess came true, editor or no editor. So did Gareth's: Python three has a median built in, in its statistics module.

00:16:30.890 --> 00:16:48.354
<v Daniel>The chapter is Playtesting, Triage and Keeping a Decisions Log, chapter sixteen in Machine Code Mayhem on Leanpub, and the exercise is Map a review. The review is in Mower Mayhem's source, and you can play the game itself free in your browser at dalex-software dot co dot uk.

00:16:48.954 --> 00:17:05.185
<v Daniel>Thank you for listening. The theme is by Dez. Dalex Software, its people and Ink and Paper are fictional, the voices are synthesised, and the show is written and produced by me, Daniel Vaughan, with AI agents, from my book Machine Code Mayhem. The games are real, and so is every fact.
